d uh so  they're incomplete and some of which  were finding over time  and this book at the time that it was  written incorporated  the best of that that research so i  think that's a real high point of the  book i think he did an excellent job  with that  another thing i really love about this  book is that it has  so many useful practices  some of which are drawn from directly  from the epicureans the classic  epicureans  some of which are taking epicurean ideas  and saying well how can we  turn this into a practice how can we  operationalize this you might say  and business speak and then some of  which are actually drawn from  other wisdom traditions saying you know  they've got something good over there  like for example buddhist uh mediterrane  loving-kindness meditation could that be  used  in an epicurean way a hiram thinks that  it can and  he does so in the work and so i think  that that's  really awesome to offer people a lot of  practices that they can try out  experiment with experience what the  results are  and then come back and reflect on it  that's very important in a work  like this another thing that i've  already mentioned several times but i  think is a really high point of the work  although i have to qualify this when i  get to the one i found problematic  there is a lot of engagement with other  traditions  of philosophy and even you know  some religious traditions like like  hinduism and and buddhism  and so i think that you know being  willing to learn  from whoever has something useful and  then incorporate it that's the sign of a  living tradition that's something that  aleister mcintyre has written quite  about you know  quite a lot about if you want your  tradition to stay alive you don't just  stick with the stuff that you've got you  poach everybody else's stuff  and you make it your own and so you know  that that's been done quite  well here um i think that there's a  really good  reinterpretation of this idea of  pleasure as an end or goal and i'm going  to read a little bit uh from the text  here he says  i don't necessarily treat pleasure as a  goal in the way that  philosophers speak of the telos the aim  or ultimate goal in life  and why because he brings up a scholar  from  harvard university matt killingworth it  indicates that we're  happiest when we're not seeking  happiness but wrapped in the moment  happiness requires focus and is a mode  of being not a  mode of seeking and so you know he he  talks about what are the implications  for this  the philosophically hedonist theory that  epicurus proposed  is um calls for a preliminary process of  introspection  discerning between unnecessary and  unnecessary desires and so  he's bringing in some ideas but he's  reworking  the idea of happiness or you know  eudaimonia  as as the end here in a way that i think  is very  thoughtful there's also a really nice  although short discussion of what the  epicureans  had to say about anger  this is something that a lot of people  struggle with today probably just as  much as  in ancient time and so this gets a you  know  fairly sustained treatment we're getting  [Music]  at least yeah five pages devoted to it  and so that that's quite good so there's  a lot of things that i think are  excellent  in this work  no work is usually perfect and there are  a few things that i do find problematic  about this work  one is just a few mistakes here and  there  which might have been the result of  trying to  simplify things for the reader you know  for example  uh calling the carvaka materialist  school  in in ancient india a hindu school it  was not an orthodox hindu school it's  you know definitely outside of that so  that's you know that's a mistake  i i don't know whether i imagine  actually  hiram knows that but it got in there  that's not that big of a deal something  that i find a bit more  problematic and and i want to say  something here that doesn't just apply  to this book  but generally applies to anything  about epicureanism that is advocating  epicureanism  and often even more broadly the hedonist  tradition  there's some bad history in there  there's some some bad history of ideas  and some bad history of religion and  you know it's it's interesting because  it's  it's distracting it doesn't need to be  in there  an epicurean can do just fine without  bashing platonism  without bashing christianity without  getting these things wrong  right um so i and this is a failure in a  lot of this literature  so you know hear him crespo falls into  that  trap as as do so many others i'll just  give you a few examples  of this you know he talks about  platonism  uh in a way that's clearly incorrect and  i i think is unfair uh he says  for example we must keep in mind that to  the platonus reason was a separate  entity a deity with a concrete identity  we hold this to be a false view there's  absolutely no reason to believe our  reasoning faculty is divine or exists in  a supernatural or eternal realm  well the plaintiffs didn't actually buy  into that  and that's sort of a misconstrual of of  platonism  in in general and you wonder well what's  what's the beef there what what are they  getting  out of that and you know it's they'd be  better off leaving that sort of stuff  behind  there's a particularly tip you know  typical bad history passage  a little bit later in the work you know  which starts with the burning of the  library of alexandria uh you know the  philosophical schools being closed and  for over a thousand years the people  succumbed to the superstition  ignorance misery and feudal slavery of  the dark ages from which we have still  not made a full recovery so i mean  that's  that's just garbage history right you  know that could be in gibbon or  whatever whatever i mean real historians  know that the dark ages did happen but  it was very  very short and christianity and you know  particularly christian monks were the  ones preserving the knowledge  expanding it getting us out of the dark  ages  so you know that sort of stuff i don't  need to belabor the point  it's just kind of silly detraction  that doesn't need to be there because  the rest of the work is really quite  good  so those are really the only things that  i found particularly  problematic about the work  so my last thoughts are usually very  brief but i'm actually going to include  a little bit from the text in this case  something that i think is actually quite  important and why i think  this book even if you don't want to like  be an epicurean  why i think if you're interested in  intentional communities and philosophy  as a way of life i think you should get  this book  so uh hiram crespo says as autonomous  value creating communities  the epicurean gardens may one day become  spaces where people  perform rites of passage according to  their authentic needs  and informed choices what he's talking  about there  is doing something like i  said people have done in other movements  and taking epicureanism and making it  part  of an intentional community life and i  think that is really  quite interesting a little bit later  in the work he says that spaces of  refuge  we create must be subcultures of  philosophy sustainable economics and  non-consumerism  and because of that the very creation of  those spaces inherently constitutes a  political  and subversive act in our consumerist  society so he has a real  program here and i think the you know uh  society of the friends of epicurus is  part of that  the entire 13th chapter is about  what he calls the matter of cultural  continuity and  he's talking about how to draw things  from other traditions  the teaching mission wisdom is of value  he talks about the society of the  friends of  epicurious and about the what he calls  the 20th  urs the people of epicurus those who  celebrate the birthday of epicurus so  this is this is in some respect  not just a self-help book but kind of a  manifesto for  a movement that is existing today and is  well worth  checking out if you're interested in  intentional life so i  you know without any qualification  recommend this  book uh it's it's a very you know  quick read but quite rich and well worth  having on your bookshelf  you
 

